#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335969 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087698 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7b6ddadb-471a-5195-a0f5-fd44bdc8f780" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Other Matrices"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06401192-8d9f-5b90-bad0-6a7cbd2a2533" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333044} {:answer/id #uuid "99a5b892-603e-567b-9580-d1d29c790f2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334346} {:answer/id #uuid "ad1bc08e-261d-5c96-9948-95bf3dfb4241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334513} {:answer/id #uuid "c6a2e2a7-2e15-5c2f-955e-cdf162b665ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334715} {:answer/id #uuid "dfac6d2f-1492-5a0a-8cd5-e7e59cffdd38" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ad1bc08e-261d-5c96-9948-95bf3dfb4241" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334513} :answer-field/id #uuid "28a23270-bbbf-5282-a183-cf8454b9420a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335314}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6f03670c-374b-5a68-bc3e-7afd1cdf1d29" :question/math-academy-id "q-68751" :question/problem "Which of the following are row vectors?\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 & 3 & -2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 0 & 4 & 1\\end{aligned}],\\,B=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 6\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$" :question/worked-solution "- $A$ is *not* a row vector, since it has more than $1$ row (it has $2$ rows).\n\n- $B$ is *not* a row vector. It has only one column (and $4$ rows), so it's a column vector.\n\n- $C$ is a row vector since it has only one row.\n\nTherefore, only $C$ is a row vector."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186335981 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096691 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c015dcaa-2155-5d80-985b-21924de92b28" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Vector Equalities"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5c86553e-0568-5bba-a07a-1304b12cdc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333832} {:answer/id #uuid "b8b88a25-3cf8-548e-bf49-e34860873e27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334608} {:answer/id #uuid "ba671f18-a3cd-5299-a2e3-925d73e1b735" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334617} {:answer/id #uuid "ced30abd-fb51-5f57-989a-ae4a80541fbc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334794} {:answer/id #uuid "e6471dc6-fde6-563c-ac28-7e9d02dede89" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334997}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5c86553e-0568-5bba-a07a-1304b12cdc8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333832} :answer-field/id #uuid "0aeeae43-d933-5707-a2c3-41e206d6908e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335254}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8c4b76bc-1da4-582d-a2ec-327744496809" :question/math-academy-id "q-69539" :question/problem "![](images/86/86aac25c634d29639329ae53209141ce81a3a82a70d42a4c2dfc6d356ee378b8.png)\n\nIn figure $ABCD$ shown above, which of the following are equal to the vector $\\overset{\\to }{DB}?$\n\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB}$\n- $\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{CD}$" :question/worked-solution "- For the $1$st expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} & =(\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DC}+\\overset{\\to }{CB} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $2$nd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{DA}+\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =\\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- For the $3$rd expression, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}-\\overset{\\to }{CA})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =(\\overset{\\to }{BA}+\\overset{\\to }{AC})+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BC}+\\overset{\\to }{CD} \\\\  & =\\overset{\\to }{BD} \\\\  & \\ne \\overset{\\to }{DB}\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186336016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186059789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f4b612ef-ce7f-5783-848d-d8ef210c9f47" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Argand Diagram Corresponding to a Complex Number"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04d6264c-87ee-59ec-a2e5-119161661d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333028} {:answer/id #uuid "0f376cd1-2258-5fe6-bb7b-f218426ef294" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333121} {:answer/id #uuid "47a18374-fce5-5721-b770-52e4b165fde7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333651} {:answer/id #uuid "9aa10e6e-b5be-5450-aa82-7ce661e38f1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334357} {:answer/id #uuid "d4be3afa-8e44-5951-8b40-54acbf8bf4fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334853}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04d6264c-87ee-59ec-a2e5-119161661d16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333028} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8139605-8097-5753-a1b4-7f8f0608d086" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335670}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "36192a12-3447-5ff2-9e37-3b54bb995a4a" :question/math-academy-id "q-69899" :question/problem "Which of the following numbers lie in the second quadrant in an Argand diagram?\n\n- $-2+\\text{i}$\n- $-3-2\\text{i}$\n- $-3+2\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "- The complex number $-2+\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-2$ and the imaginary part $y=1.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-2,1)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3-2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=-2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,-2)$, which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n- The complex number $-3+2\\text{i}$ has the real part $x=-3$ and the imaginary part $y=2.$ So, it corresponds to the point $(-3,2)$, which lies in the second quadrant.\n\n![](images/d6/d683b808bee4b7893952adbaf87940066f9ff7d61c99ce7cc7e05fc9a4fbe02f.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338767 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119790 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd9b0f65-d8a3-57c4-88c5-2738b0938c0d" :knowledge-point/title "Solving for a Coefficient in a 2x2 Linear System Using Cramer's Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e6b5bf1-3c33-555c-81a5-2d4ea062e971" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336477} {:answer/id #uuid "36c2e7a6-c35a-5557-b5e3-1a1865808b1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336540} {:answer/id #uuid "44afc8f2-d374-5ba1-857b-e6d2ddd04cfe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336673} {:answer/id #uuid "6bacb049-bc41-5c9b-b021-9c5d4b530cf2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336984} {:answer/id #uuid "c19fc178-f0dc-5c75-9951-42642f50bf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337752}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c19fc178-f0dc-5c75-9951-42642f50bf72" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337752} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6db3a18-f1f8-5aa4-8236-f342d70e462d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338612}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e21820bb-bc15-5fe3-a578-6f44daf46f1f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70252" :question/problem "Let $⟨-1,y⟩$ be the solution of the system of equations below. \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x+ky=-4 \\\\ 3x-4y=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWhich of the following equations could be solved to find the value of $k?$\n\n- $|\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|=-2|\\begin{aligned}-4 & k \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}|$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|=|\\begin{aligned}-4 & k \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}|$\n- $|\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|=-|\\begin{aligned}-4 & k \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}|$" :question/worked-solution "The determinant of the coefficient matrix is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAccording to Cramer's rule, the value of $x$ is given by $x=\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D},$ where\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{D}_{x} & =|\\begin{aligned}-4 & k \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}|.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\frac{{D}_{x}}{D} \\\\ -1 & =\\frac{|\\begin{aligned}-4 & k \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}|}{|\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}|} \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & k \\\\ 3 & -4\\end{aligned}| & =-|\\begin{aligned}-4 & k \\\\ 5 & -4\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338801 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186176055 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6a18aadb-3e8a-54d7-a5d7-e78208e77675" :knowledge-point/title "Describing Properties of the Cotangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15129ab1-4c46-56cd-9295-d5604da9b82a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336232} {:answer/id #uuid "1f18694d-3bfc-5660-9cf9-33f02dcb9fa6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336319} {:answer/id #uuid "605f040a-a7b3-52da-b5bf-64a199155157" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336904} {:answer/id #uuid "72fb3c80-d6b2-5e94-bae7-692fa5b792c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337067} {:answer/id #uuid "ea50c4ab-53d3-59d3-915e-68d350aa77ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338077}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72fb3c80-d6b2-5e94-bae7-692fa5b792c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337067} :answer-field/id #uuid "614d9852-d75d-56d9-af20-89203dd46a06" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4a752d35-73b8-55ea-bb50-4d11f60be65b" :question/math-academy-id "q-70669" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x?$ Assume $n$ is an integer.\n\nI. The function has no minimum value. $$\n\nII. $x=-4\\pi $ is in the domain of the function. $$\n\nIII. $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a zero of the function." :question/worked-solution "Let's plot the graph of $y=cot⁡x.$\n\n![](images/8b/8bc549c68a3bc5b7989ac1835e4bb58183066c7797264b8cccd4f3fd2c895056.png)\n\nNow, let's go through each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function has no minimum or maximum value. $$\n\n- Statement II is false. The function is defined for all real numbers except the values $n\\pi $ for any integer $n$, thus it is not defined for $x=-4\\pi .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The zeros of $y=cot⁡x$ occur at $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+n\\pi ,$ for any integer $n.$ Therefore, $x=-\\frac{3\\pi }{2}$ is a zero of the function."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110571 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "67692569-e3a4-51e8-99ac-79d8b3081363" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Sine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f535de0-5f7e-5f07-b728-e8b69b78ce40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336485} {:answer/id #uuid "3e3397d2-3bb7-5a5f-abab-6346b796162c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336618} {:answer/id #uuid "9cc08e91-a405-5130-9e40-ad42774323ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337448} {:answer/id #uuid "a44457ee-b891-55a9-9e35-e87ad6e00f66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337527} {:answer/id #uuid "e75d325c-d187-58b5-8950-374a69f3c245" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e3397d2-3bb7-5a5f-abab-6346b796162c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336618} :answer-field/id #uuid "6936c6f5-7e26-51e6-bf78-eb7cdfeb806b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338462}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc9d880f-4b58-5f10-97af-057cfca9838a" :question/math-academy-id "q-70714" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$\n- $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,$ meaning $a=\\operatorname{sin}⁡b.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points.  \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{2},1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is $y\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(1,\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$  does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338817 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2e1ab193-b02a-50a0-b500-2119148de59d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336475} {:answer/id #uuid "5cfde7e4-e0e4-5b33-8c9e-8218ac614d30" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336874} {:answer/id #uuid "c419087d-6fa7-5da6-9af2-eabc4eb07799" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337773} {:answer/id #uuid "dcef36a2-a594-5dd2-ab3f-4eb145a8488f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337954} {:answer/id #uuid "e2bd42e1-5e26-550f-8177-6f0d312e764d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dcef36a2-a594-5dd2-ab3f-4eb145a8488f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337954} :answer-field/id #uuid "eae011b5-ed3d-57f7-a362-b4df68dbfbed" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338686}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "5a1259cd-784d-5b09-bc57-e1c65f1d431f" :question/math-academy-id "q-70768" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$\n- $(-1,\\pi )$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{6},\\frac{1}{2})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{3\\pi }{4},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{4})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-1,\\pi ).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\pi ,-1)$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\pi $ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-1,\\pi )$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110575 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "af0b18ca-54a2-5a24-a670-faa311096abc" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Points that Lie on the Curve of the Inverse Cosine Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05402959-779b-53bf-8c25-5fcdc1809c24" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336073} {:answer/id #uuid "07e00a1b-f76f-5803-838a-2f68bc4933a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336096} {:answer/id #uuid "3c65e134-8daf-5a9c-9f37-f27e9a7bea71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336603} {:answer/id #uuid "c92234e6-3ee0-5903-8ef9-a557277b9d88" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186337810} {:answer/id #uuid "ec645aea-f8fd-5652-b07f-3d6a05ca7307" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338093}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3c65e134-8daf-5a9c-9f37-f27e9a7bea71" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336603} :answer-field/id #uuid "cab890f8-e77d-5a65-952c-4f9f7b758939" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186338640}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7b495bf5-5746-5288-a51e-cecce774eb37" :question/math-academy-id "q-70770" :question/problem "Which of the following points lies on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x?$\n\n- $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$\n- $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$\n- $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$" :question/worked-solution "In order for a point $(a,b)$ to lie on the graph of the function $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ the following conditions must hold:\n\n- The reflected point $(b,a)$ must lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$\n\n- The $y$-value of the point must lie in the range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x,$ which is $y\\in [0,\\pi ].$\n\nWith this in mind, let's check each of the points. \n\n- First, we consider the point $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{\\pi }{4},-\\frac{1}{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$  $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{1}{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Next, we consider the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(-\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{1}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ does **not** lie within this range.   $\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the point $(\\frac{1}{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{3})$ does **not** lie on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$\n\n- Finally, we consider the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ We note the following:\n\n- The point $(\\frac{5\\pi }{6},-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.\\,✓$\n\n- The range of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is $y\\in [0,\\pi ],$ and $y=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}$ lies within this range. $\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ lies on the graph of $y=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186338819 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110587 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b834b64-9679-56c2-b5e9-ee26a54b5e13" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Values in the Domain of the Inverse Tangent Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f37a2f4-ae1d-5dfa-b9b5-214fca1429f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336177} {:answer/id #uuid "53a4e3d5-f09d-5fcc-8a7a-5f9a0eef9b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186336807} {:answer/id #uuid "6af11d36-3423-5212-8673-c652921498