principle. In this case, $c=-5.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348281 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152193 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b0829d4a-239a-5a8e-9c8e-3cdc76a993bc" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Constant Multiple Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "565ac935-420d-5f95-a28d-92456faced76" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346084} {:answer/id #uuid "991744d5-719c-562a-98e8-cbd3d093ac57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346685} {:answer/id #uuid "9afcf3c3-ebc6-5837-b7cb-42137df13251" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346706} {:answer/id #uuid "9f998980-a9b5-5af7-8538-ea271ed86de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346745} {:answer/id #uuid "f5cf9711-8831-5d9e-8592-607bdbb062ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9f998980-a9b5-5af7-8538-ea271ed86de1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346745} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a64b2bc-95cd-52af-bee8-0963efc75e7f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347845}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "107897db-d4be-5207-adc1-b404c9d01f49" :question/math-academy-id "q-79777" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$\n- $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the function ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, and $c$ is a constant, then $y(x)=c{y}_{1}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)=-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=-3.$\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the product of a non-constant function and a known solution.\n\n- The function $y(x)=\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{2}$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. In this case, $c=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348285 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1472b95c-69ea-5b14-8dd4-89aef46f7fe9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345524} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0b76c8-9fcf-5df6-8c74-835bcbbfa612" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345860} {:answer/id #uuid "4ff6d3eb-665a-5929-b7c0-8b79a662ccb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346033} {:answer/id #uuid "e652fc59-a495-5990-be19-3d1a1a916abe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347382} {:answer/id #uuid "ed65c49d-dd46-5594-86e2-db77968435c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4ff6d3eb-665a-5929-b7c0-8b79a662ccb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346033} :answer-field/id #uuid "97d56d4c-d604-591b-aee3-dc06c79970cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb140c30-bdb5-5662-90f1-f05ca3e626cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-79800" :question/problem "Given that ${y}_{1}(x)={x}^{2}$ and ${y}_{2}(x)={x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear, second-order ODE, then according to the superposition principle which of the following must also be a solution to the equation?\n\n- $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)$\n- $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$" :question/worked-solution "According to the superposition principle, if the functions ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ are solutions to a homogeneous, linear differential equation, then $y(x)={y}_{1}(x)+{y}_{2}(x)$ is also a solution to the equation.\n\nLet's consider each option:\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x={x}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle. The superposition principle does not extend to the quotients of the known solutions.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}(1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)={x}^{2}+{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is a solution of the equation by the superposition principle.\n\n- The function $y(x)={x}^{2}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is **not** guaranteed to be a solution of the equation by the superposition principle because $\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ is not one of the known solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186348296 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152192 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "42bc95a3-4e0c-5487-8427-31cee0ecf80b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Solutions of Linear ODEs Using the Sum Rule"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "399cfc3e-0e98-531e-8101-0c3517202d0f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186345842} {:answer/id #uuid "5c5d5db9-56b1-580b-9ac9-53a7414e616b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346139} {:answer/id #uuid "66e8b755-dcc3-5191-b6e6-9ac03363fc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346251} {:answer/id #uuid "7a9e482e-853e-5ee4-9487-d2fdaefb7d00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346423} {:answer/id #uuid "d42faa0f-d1bb-56c6-b164-a60c6f94f831" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186347207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66e8b755-dcc3-5191-b6e6-9ac03363fc00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186346251} :answer-field/id #uuid "f2674e3a-87e7-58d5-b756-a0d1604a52a5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ide