ng the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 0 & 3 \\\\ 1 & -4 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 0 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(5,-1),(0,4),(-3,-5).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351375 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092224 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "20f4ef93-3e7b-58ec-992e-3a62b12b74e2" :knowledge-point/title "Using a Matrix That Represents a Rotation of 180 Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25878e3e-4660-56b1-aebc-5cf486c84d7d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348769} {:answer/id #uuid "4e92eff1-2f22-58c8-909d-3a69618c4e67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349159} {:answer/id #uuid "85db19be-aa9b-5ed6-999a-6d25c79e13bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349669} {:answer/id #uuid "f179df73-8f35-56c5-8bc7-5ae38d792a66" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350557} {:answer/id #uuid "faf2b2ad-b924-585c-afc9-e1870c44b16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faf2b2ad-b924-585c-afc9-e1870c44b16c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350657} :answer-field/id #uuid "31b5282d-1b98-5d48-8d34-e38fc8f72d36" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eca92846-b208-5714-9d9f-4947d539dbcc" :question/math-academy-id "q-81819" :question/problem "A triangle $S$ has vertices at $(4,4),(-3,-3),(-1,7).$ Which of the following are coordinates of the vertices of ${S}^{′},$ the image of $S$ under the counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ about the origin?\n\n- $(3,-3)$\n- $(-4,-4)$\n- $(1,-7)$" :question/worked-solution "The counterclockwise rotation ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by an angle of ${180}^{∘}$ is given by the matrix\n\n$$\nR=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the image of $S$ under the action of ${R}_{{180}^{∘}},$ we first create a matrix $X$ containing all of the vertices of $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}X & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under ${R}_{{180}^{∘}}$ by calculating the matrix product $RX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}RX & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & -3 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3 & 7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & -7\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective vertices of ${S}^{′}$ are $(-4,-4),(3,3),(1,-7).$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351421 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e08d1ad-f5c0-5097-9890-f0ab89b66086" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348566} {:answer/id #uuid "31280c8e-36d5-5edb-8935-400e163c6254" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348882} {:answer/id #uuid "5b0ed777-5ab0-5b13-827c-7d063fe7e6c4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349284} {:answer/id #uuid "d973a507-2ddd-5e50-833f-ee66a5642171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350352} {:answer/id #uuid "ed5a03b2-59e7-58c6-9098-8c0824620a6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350521}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d973a507-2ddd-5e50-833f-ee66a5642171" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350352} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6188e16-8fdf-508e-9b0f-8d7f586a31a2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351142}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0e6a1e4f-2315-5270-b1fa-4652e5933f97" :question/math-academy-id "q-82396" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-8 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2},{w}_{3}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0,\\,{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0,\\,{w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=-10-3+0=-13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-18-2=-20\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=2-1-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0-1+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351422 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1808b334-76b3-52df-a1a6-8b27cf9bde32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348649} {:answer/id #uuid "1f42a2b4-971f-5282-bafa-57f6edbf33b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348719} {:answer/id #uuid "95671ee1-ea34-5b9e-b055-16b7e130dd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349788} {:answer/id #uuid "9f0bd696-9fc4-5142-916d-9086d9ae01bb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349865} {:answer/id #uuid "ce07f604-e928-5e49-bbf0-cb8821612bd7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1808b334-76b3-52df-a1a6-8b27cf9bde32" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348649} :answer-field/id #uuid "248c40ff-0114-5468-a84e-a4a88929f614" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350774}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7632fcb-d145-5958-9540-44c860cfa586" :question/math-academy-id "q-82397" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=-1+3-1-1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1+0-2+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+2-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=2-1-1=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $V$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -8\\end{aligned}]=-15+24=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $V$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351423 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "495e79d0-5695-5b1d-b180-8255ddd180d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349112} {:answer/id #uuid "4f8f838d-c58e-52c5-9a16-9f6273240074" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349165} {:answer/id #uuid "4fd9369b-ea61-5a1d-ac7f-55fd79648d4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349169} {:answer/id #uuid "9477547c-7d95-50de-b89c-0ff5612d9aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349774} {:answer/id #uuid "b50a8836-0fef-5adc-a47b-d13e1dab0baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350049}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9477547c-7d95-50de-b89c-0ff5612d9aad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349774} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e054f01-0c8d-5ba5-b957-74b1a6c94ad6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350892}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2c1e164f-769d-52d4-a09c-f8474e7b2368" :question/math-academy-id "q-82401" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$\n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}}$, respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=1+1=2\\ne 1 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $U$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=1+0=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=0+1=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthonormal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351471 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61be30a1-e0f8-5e75-b806-749de2961190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349341} {:answer/id #uuid "84316694-dae1-5f54-aebd-e653a190107b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349653} {:answer/id #uuid "94cdc7c9-8342-50ab-9bea-d6611c359cb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349780} {:answer/id #uuid "a559cc25-0dd3-5186-9ef2-495f6b774679" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349920} {:answer/id #uuid "cbbaf352-55b2-5f1b-9c0b-327d64fe6e4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350238}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61be30a1-e0f8-5e75-b806-749de2961190" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349341} :answer-field/id #uuid "1c5a073c-52a4-505b-b138-c4213c01bfe1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350760}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d21c33a8-a8c0-5b77-a079-4c787c111b5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-8296" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5(kx)+2(ky) \\\\ 3(ky)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}k(5x+2y) \\\\ k(3y)\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}5x+2y \\\\ 3y\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})+2({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{1}+2{y}_{1} \\\\ 3{y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}5{x}_{2}+2{y}_{2} \\\\ 3{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351472 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186087746 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d8c04b23-e341-57e9-a072-985e4b536e5a" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Linear Transformations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c8885d8-6682-5fa2-8910-913ea7fa4354" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348693} {:answer/id #uuid "1eb9e24d-e09f-5514-99f5-7f780feb6c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348715} {:answer/id #uuid "3e386808-f929-5075-847b-8e26ef770089" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349014} {:answer/id #uuid "78bbafdc-71b8-57e8-be89-9a17e0e6c1a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349563} {:answer/id #uuid "da3a295e-ce40-59ab-96ab-0c122163f211" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350360}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1eb9e24d-e09f-5514-99f5-7f780feb6c5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348715} :answer-field/id #uuid "43c3161f-b901-5adc-b467-8ed60dcbe261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350821}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5104f251-39bc-5c29-bbcf-96e73f6b8fe5" :question/math-academy-id "q-8297" :question/problem "Let \n$T:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}].$\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- $T(kv)=kT(v)$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vector $v$ and real scalar $k$\n- $T({v}_{1}+{v}_{2})=T({v}_{1})+T({v}_{2})$ for any real $2\\times 1$ column vectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$\n- $T$ is a linear transformation" :question/worked-solution "A linear transformation $T$ satisfies two properties:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}]=kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}], \\\\  & T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}]=T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the first condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}kx \\\\ ky\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3ky \\\\ 2kx\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =k[\\begin{aligned}3y \\\\ 2x\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =kT[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Checking the second condition, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}+{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}+{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3({y}_{1}+{y}_{2}) \\\\ 2({x}_{1}+{x}_{2})\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{1} \\\\ 2{x}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}3{y}_{2} \\\\ 2{x}_{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1} \\\\ {y}_{1}\\end{aligned}]+T[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{2}\\end{aligned}].\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\text{T}$ is a linear transformation.\n\nAs a result, all three statements are true."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133843 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ee1838df-f1d6-5bc4-908c-b96e2ad577c9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthogonal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19628589-c585-50da-a08f-d16156b30654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348658} {:answer/id #uuid "32242d4f-c08f-5634-bb7c-a044ed166cca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348892} {:answer/id #uuid "5b245e85-64c4-5b15-b9e5-cf3890016d6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349286} {:answer/id #uuid "60abd1fa-0da0-5751-abb2-866a646578fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349327} {:answer/id #uuid "cfd94aba-1787-5be4-942a-24258edda1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350274}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cfd94aba-1787-5be4-942a-24258edda1da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350274} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6e9a62b-01e6-5967-8440-2d30c852b68d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351052}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3593d781-0056-56f1-8b48-1ee74f8fd0fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-83719" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthogonal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-6 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2},{u}_{3}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthogonal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0,\\,{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0,\\,{u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0,\\,{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0,\\,{v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0.$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]=-1+0+1=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{3} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=0+0+0=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthogonal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]=12+0+1=13\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthogonal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ notice that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}8 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -10\\end{aligned}]=-40+40=0 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $W$ is orthogonal.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351542 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133781 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ed5c8af7-e617-5e8c-be01-45a26eb17d4d" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Whether Sets are Orthonormal"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a4f28f2-d974-5a65-8213-04795e7de605" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348973} {:answer/id #uuid "5e34ff4d-8f7c-5ec9-8897-24713cfde0e2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349311} {:answer/id #uuid "6b9e1ec6-8e0b-5da1-9fd6-8d626d78ba4c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $V$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349438} {:answer/id #uuid "cd3681bc-7477-528f-821a-19436df269a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350250} {:answer/id #uuid "cdab980a-d6f5-5611-ade3-bc4a1413c279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$V$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350257}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd3681bc-7477-528f-821a-19436df269a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$U$ and $W$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350250} :answer-field/id #uuid "cded3021-7d49-58db-a464-fd687f0c58fc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351066}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "757ea74d-034b-5cf5-b116-1f66ebbe72c0" :question/math-academy-id "q-83745" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are orthonormal?\n\n- $U={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $V={[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}]}$\n- $W={[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]}$" :question/worked-solution "Let's denote the vectors of the given sets as \n${{u}_{1},{u}_{2}},$ \n${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}},$ and \n${{w}_{1},{w}_{2}},$ respectively. \n\nThe sets $U,V,$ and $W$ are orthonormal if the following conditions on each set hold:\n\n- ${u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1}=1 \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2}=0 \\\\ {v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{3}=0\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}=1 \\\\ {v}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}=1 \\\\ {v}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\n- ${w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}=1 \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}=1.\\end{aligned}$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets in turn.\n\n- For the set $U,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=-\\frac{6}{13}+\\frac{6}{13}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{1}\\cdot {u}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{13}+\\frac{9}{13}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {u}_{2}\\cdot {u}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{9}{13}+\\frac{4}{13}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the set $U$ is orthonormal.\n\n- For the set $V,$ notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]=0+1+8=9\\ne 0 & \\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $V$ is not an orthonormal set.\n\n- For the set $W,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{2}{5}-\\frac{2}{5}=0 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{4}{5}+\\frac{1}{5}=1 & ✓ \\\\ {w}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2} & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}=1 & ✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $W$ is an orthonormal set.\n\nTherefore, we conclude that the correct answer is \"$U$ and $W$ only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351543 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "120fab5a-7a2e-524f-959b-d51035c63e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348601} {:answer/id #uuid "4eac6fc5-516a-54d0-a870-566797eef57b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349160} {:answer/id #uuid "bf2aab15-3795-57cb-96d1-6a1357e369ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350143} {:answer/id #uuid "ee203bcc-11df-5642-a2fb-37f65be8304b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350527} {:answer/id #uuid "fda5d663-19cc-5d86-a753-5c963c0408d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350679}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "120fab5a-7a2e-524f-959b-d51035c63e57" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348601} :answer-field/id #uuid "de3d86c1-9453-5879-b8eb-16a995edd2f6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351096}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "97ab1308-2ae6-5488-8b7b-f352f2f86ff6" :question/math-academy-id "q-83746" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The number of hearts in a standard deck of cards\n- The number of diamonds obtained when $7$ cards are randomly selected from a standard deck of cards\n- The number of times a card is selected from a standard deck of cards until a King appears" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. The number of hearts in a particular complete deck of cards is not the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and its possible values can be listed in a sequence: $0,1,2,3,4,5,6,7.$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can count its possible values in a sequence: $1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351546 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4aec78c4-7503-5290-ab1e-e331abe9d6da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349132} {:answer/id #uuid "693eaeb1-6a33-509d-b224-d974178cc9bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349421} {:answer/id #uuid "6c3da218-06de-5858-a402-9028bb376e02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349445} {:answer/id #uuid "907e3e43-5694-5205-ace7-54016bcd6661" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349744} {:answer/id #uuid "d93b18f2-af63-503c-95a8-606c11f0e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350351}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d93b18f2-af63-503c-95a8-606c11f0e71b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350351} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae8581a5-51ed-50d3-b3df-f1f3ea274f70" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351007}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "423a76f9-5b29-5c2a-b8cc-b13643f5126d" :question/math-academy-id "q-83758" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The average height of students in a particular class\n- The number of blue sugar-coated chocolates in a randomly selected bag of candy\n- The number of free-throws Sally makes in a carnival game where she attempts $3$ free-throws" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and we can list its possible values in a sequence: $0,1,2,3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351547 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c70da7a-f007-5390-a37a-d774dcdfe2f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348556} {:answer/id #uuid "3895f578-77b1-5d93-9849-a14e63e92d39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348958} {:answer/id #uuid "6e1ab1a4-be93-53ac-9254-3efcc42eeebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186349457} {:answer/id #uuid "edbae502-5baf-5ba6-8893-4fd6c0720b00" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350522} {:answer/id #uuid "f472f1a7-cd5f-5b59-ad57-71395930c414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350586}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f472f1a7-cd5f-5b59-ad57-71395930c414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186350586} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6262073-530d-5639-90cb-886bbbc814b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186351050}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "bf0e1a41-c363-549e-b4b4-5dc282d5c751" :question/math-academy-id "q-83761" :question/problem "Which of the following are discrete random variables?\n\n- The weight of a randomly selected student\n- The amount of water in a randomly selected student's water bottle\n- The number of books borrowed by a randomly selected library user in a particular month" :question/worked-solution "A random variable is a variable whose value is determined by the outcome of a trial or experiment.\n\nA *discrete* random variable is a random variable\n\n- that has finitely many possible values, or\n\n- whose possible values can be listed in a sequence: ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},{x}_{3},…$\n\nRemember that a real interval (e.g. $(a,b)$ or $[a,b]$) cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given statements.\n\n- Statement I does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, the possible values are real numbers that cannot be listed in a sequence.\n\n- Statement II does *not* describe a discrete random variable. Although its value is determined by the result of an experiment, we cannot list the possible real-numbered values in a sequence.\n\n- Statement III describes a discrete random variable. Its value is determined by the result of an experiment, and it has finitely many possible values: $0,1,2,3,…$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186351548 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186105817 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2f7256c7-afea-5c33-a859-623b0531e88c" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Discrete Random Variables"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "02471471-99b3-5b2a-990d-3d2d43f3a363" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186348473} {:answer/id #uuid "32e61107-a668-5b74-af62-61c18b847444" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answe